    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d235">

                <pb facs="0318" n="214" xml:id="pg214"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3965a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Shanghai (Cunningham)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-04T18:00:00-05:00">February 4, 1932—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>On information that may or may not be correct, we believe that Japanese infantry
                    to the extent of 4,000 may possibly have already been landed at Woosung; that
                    these are probably the advance guard of the movement of a Japanese Division. If
                    they land at Woosung, it would seem quite possible that they will move upon the
                    Chinese forces at Chapei along the railway line from Woosung to Shanghai, in
                    which case there would be danger of defeated Chinese troops being forced into
                    the American sector of the International Settlement.</p>
                <p>This is sent to you for your confidential information and that of the commanders
                    of the neutral forces.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
